INT. FREIGHT DEPOT - LOADING FLOOR - DAY

HANDHELD. A cheap camera, wobbling, date stamp in the corner. Pallets, shrink-wrap, a forklift beeping somewhere. A banner: "SAFETY FIRST - 212 DAYS."

ON CAMERA: HALVORSEN, 50s, shift supervisor, clipboard, lanyard, mustache.

HALVORSEN
(adjusting the camera strap, grinning)
Is it recording? The red light's on. Okay. Day two hundred and twelve. Everything's on schedule. We love schedules.

REPORTER (O.S.)
(laughing)
Tell them what you're shipping.

HALVORSEN
(tapping a crate with his pen)
Boots, blankets, tinned peaches. Humanitarian, all of it. Dull as dishwater.

MAYA OKONKWO
(coming into frame, a box on her hip, tugging her gloves off)
He says "dull" like it's a compliment. We had a pallet of tinned peaches yesterday that I swear wasn't there on the manifest.

HALVORSEN
(waving the clipboard)
The manifest is a suggestion.

DEV
(rolling a hand truck past, not stopping)
Maya, your mum's on speaker. Phone's in the break room.

ON PHONE: "MUM: where are u. news is on. call me"

MAYA OKONKWO
(pulling out her own phone, thumbing it, holding it up to the lens)
She sends the same text every time there's weather on the other side of the world.

ON PHONE: "MAYA: working. love you. stop watching it."

INTERCUT - FLOOR AND MUM'S KITCHEN

MUM (V.O.)
(through the phone, tinny, a kettle shrieking behind her)
Don't tell me to stop watching. There is a man on the television pointing at a map and he said your port.

MAYA OKONKWO
(turning her back to the camera, lowering her voice)
Mum. It's a warehouse in a suburb. Nobody is pointing at it.

MUM (V.O.)
(the kettle clicking off)
Then why is there a truck at the gate that isn't yours?

MAYA OKONKWO
(looking up, eyes going to the far dock door)
Hold on.

ANGLE ON the far dock. A flatbed, no logo, idling. Two men in dark jackets stand beside it. A third, KORVAC, 40s, grey stubble, steps down.

HALVORSEN
(whispering, lowering the camera slightly)
He told me she'd be here Thursday. She's not due until Thursday.

DEV
(arriving beside them, voice flat)
He said she. I thought you meant the shipment.

HALVORSEN
(a nervous laugh)
He means the shipment. She means the shipment. Everyone means the shipment.

KORVAC
(striding up, flashing a laminated card)
Inspection. All hands to the front. Phones in the bin, please.

MAYA OKONKWO
(into the phone, not moving)
Mum, I'll call you back.

MUM (V.O.)
(sharply)
Don't hang up. He's not on the news. He's in your building.

ON PHONE: "MUM: dont put it in the bin"

KORVAC
(pointing at the lens)
And turn that off.

HALVORSEN
(stepping in front of the lens, hand raised)
Sir, it's a documentary. We're cleared.

KORVAC
(quietly, reaching past him)
Not by me.

The camera drops. The frame tilts to a ceiling, to a boot, to a crate stencilled "PEACHES" with its lid already pried loose and something other than peaches inside.

DEV
(off screen, barely audible)
Maya. Don't look in the box.

CUT TO BLACK.
